; minimal -- the core
;
; Kurt reads this file when it starts, before anything else: its declarations are the core that
; every file and the shell begin with. It is never loaded (`load minimal` is an error), and its
; symbols are frozen: no file can change them. Only two internal things are made in `kurt.py`
; itself (`initial_kb`): the parse rule of `local` (below) and the marker of bound variables.

;; syntax
brackets ( )                                 ; for grouping during parsing
infix    ","  5  4                           ; for lists and tuples, right associative: `(a, b, c)` is `(a, (b, c))`
                                             ; (quoted, because a comma separates declarations)
const    ","                                 ; the fixed tuple/list constructor
infix    " " 90 90                           ; space for function applications, binds most tightly
                                             ; (`f(x)` without a space binds more tightly still, 95:
                                             ; `inv det(A)` is `inv (det A)`)

const true                                   ; declare constant symbol
bool  true                                   ; true has type bool
infix implies 13 12                          ; logical implies, right associative (since 13>12)
infix and     16 16                          ; and operator
bool  implies 0 1 2, and 0 1 2               ; both take and give booleans
const implies, and                           ; both are constants
flat  and                                    ; conjunctions need no nesting
sym   and                                    ; `A and B` is the same as `B and A`
; `EXPR local "label"` marks a label as not exported by `load` -- a special parse rule, no
; declaration (see `local_led` in `kurt.py`)
;
; the symbols declared here are frozen: no file can change them (e.g. `sym implies`), and every
; symbol starting with `$` or `%` is a variable by its name (`$x` a term, `%A` a formula)

;; inference rules genuinely hard-coded in `kurt.py` (not `use` axioms you could remove)
;
; - "top-intro": `true` is always derivable -- see `derive_expr`
; - "impl-elim" (modus ponens): given `$A` and `$A implies $B` in the theory, `$B` is
;   derivable -- see `impl_elim`; this also covers plain "restating" an already-proven
;   fact, via `impl_elim`'s premise-less-implication case (case 2 there)
; - "and-intro": given `$A` and `$B` separately provable, `$A and $B` is derivable, and
;   a claimed conjunction is split into its conjuncts, each derived separately -- see
;   `eval_expression` and the conjunction-splitting case in `derive_expr`
; - "impl-intro" / "not-intro": *not* standalone axioms here -- they are the effect of
;   closing an `assume` block (see `eval_done`'s 'assume' case): assuming `$A` and
;   deriving `$B` gives `$A implies $B`; additionally deriving `false` gives `not $A`
; - "forall-intro" / "exists-elim": the effect of closing a `let` / `pick` block (see
;   `eval_done`, `eval_pick`)
; - "by calc": with `calc on`, a comparison of numbers that computes to true, or a claim
;   that computes to a fact -- for the symbols a theory binds to the calculator
;
; every step is checked again by the kernel (`kernel_verify`, doc/kurt-soundness.md §9)
;
; NOT hard-coded, despite once being drafted here: a general "restatement" schema
; (`$A implies $A`) and a general "impl-intro" schema
; (`($A implies $B) implies ($A implies $B)`) as bare axioms -- neither is reachable as
; a standalone fact (e.g. a bare `A implies A` does not derive from nothing). Prove a
; specific instance instead, with `show`/`proof`/`assume`/`qed` (see `keywords/`,
; lessons 08-proof and 18-assume). `not`/`false` themselves also aren't declared here at
; all -- see `prop.kurt` for those, and for a general, always-available "not-intro".

;; meanings the engine implements for symbols that theories declare
;
; nothing has such a meaning by its name: a trusted theory assigns it with `builtin` (doc/kurt-
; doc.md §10) -- prop.kurt `builtin iff equivalence, or disjunction, not negation, false falsum`,
; equality.kurt `builtin = equality`, and the calculator's operations, e.g. numbers.kurt `builtin
; + add, * multiply, ...` and matrix.kurt `builtin [ matrix` (computed with `calc on`). The
; quantifiers too: logic.kurt declares `forall`/`exists` with `builtin forall universal, exists
; existential` -- without `load logic` there are no quantifiers, and no `let`/`pick`.

;; substitution operator
arity  sub 3                                 ; operator for substitutions
bindop sub                                   ; first arg must be a variable that is bound

; The minimal core deliberately has no aliases. `prop.kurt` supplies the familiar aliases for
; propositional syntax (`⊤`, `⇒`, `∧`, ...), and `logic.kurt` the quantifiers with `∀` and `∃`.
; Keeping the glyphs theory-local lets a deeply embedded object language declare them with
; another meaning.
